---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const farmEconCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Thin margins, big variance', note: 'Farms are price-takers (commodity markets) with weather-driven output — the margin×volume squeeze (the business lesson\'s unit economics) plus a volatility no factory knows. Risk management IS farm management.' },
    { math: '2 · Subsidies shape the field', note: 'OECD countries support agriculture to the tune of ~$300B+/yr (price supports, direct payments, insurance) — distorting trade and land prices while buffering farmers; every farm\'s accounts are written partly in policy (the policy-cycle lesson, agrarian chapter).' },
    { math: '3 · The cost ladder', note: 'Land (rent or purchase — usually the largest), seed/feed, machinery (capital — the NPV lesson sizes a combine), labour, finance. Commodity farmers survive on cost position; differentiate (organic, direct sales) and the price-taker constraint relaxes.' },
    { math: '4 · The cycles', note: 'Commodity price cycles (planting decisions respond to last year\'s price — the cobweb model), land booms and busts, and the generational succession problem: farming\'s capital intensity meets its demographic cliff (average farmer age ~58 in the US).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/farm-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Farm economics is the <strong>business lesson run under weather and policy</strong>: the same
    unit-economics spine (margins, fixed costs, capital budgeting) with commodity prices and
    climate as uncontrollable swings — hence insurance, hedging and subsidies as the sector\'s
    load-bearing institutions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="margins and variance, subsidies, the cost ladder, the cycles" widget="equationStep" config={farmEconCfg} />

  <h2>Tools and honest numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hedging works:</strong> futures and forward contracts lock prices before harvest — the finance lesson\'s derivatives as honest insurance, not speculation; basis risk (local vs futures price) is the craft.</li>
    <li><strong>Crop insurance is engineered actuarial machinery:</strong> yield and revenue products priced by risk zone; subsidised premiums (US ~60%) — the single largest farm-programme channel now.</li>
    <li><strong>Value capture vs volume:</strong> direct-to-consumer, organic premiums (yield penalty vs price premium — the certified-organic transition\'s 3-year no-man\'s-land), agritourism — the strategy lesson\'s differentiation, farmed.</li>
    <li><strong>Scale keeps compounding:</strong> the mid-size family farm is squeezed between commodity scale and niche margin — the structure every rural-policy debate is really about.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> USDA ERS farm-sector accounts; OECD Agricultural Policy Monitoring; the economics and business ladders.</p>
</TopicPage>
